-  "prompt": "Support has these reports about signing in and out:\n\n1. When anyone signs out, everybody else is signed out as well: every device, every account.\n2. Password-reset links say they are no longer valid by the time people open them, unless they click within a minute of asking. A link that has already been used keeps working.\n3. After resetting a password, a laptop that was already signed in stays signed in.\n4. Asking for a second reset link leaves the first one working.\n5. When one account is hit by many wrong sign-in attempts, everybody gets \"too many attempts\". The same happens with reset requests: one address asking repeatedly blocks resets for everyone.\n6. The \"remember me\" box has no effect: every sign-in lasts a month, even on a shared computer.\n\nExpected behaviour:\n\n- Signing out ends that session and only that one: the same account's other devices and other people stay signed in. Signing out an unknown or already-ended session is harmless, and a fresh sign-in afterwards starts a new session.\n- A sign-in without \"remember me\" lasts 24 hours; with it, 30 days. Past that, the session is refused.\n- A password-reset link is usable for 60 minutes after it is sent, and only once. Requesting a new link retires every earlier unused link of that account, and no other account's. Completing a reset ends every session of that account and nobody else's; afterwards the new password signs in and the old one does not. A request for an unknown address still completes silently, with no link sent. Addresses are matched without regard to letter case.\n- Sign-in attempts are limited per account (by address, ignoring letter case), so attempts on one address never lock another account out. A throttled account is refused even with the right password until its limit recovers, and can still request and complete a password reset. Reset requests are limited per address in the same way, independently of sign-in attempts.\n\nRules for this task (a violation di