heir names.\n- Replays: uploading a key this workspace has already imported returns the result of that first import — the same issues, in the same order, with `replayed: true` — and creates nothing, whatever the rows now say. A different key is a new import even when the rows are identical. Keys are per workspace: the same key in another workspace is unrelated. Replays must work across restarts, so imports are persisted; a freshly migrated database (the way the test suite builds one) must have whatever you add.\n- Announcements: a successful import publishes exactly one `issues.imported` event for the batch and no `issue.created`; a replay publishes nothing. The batch event must give the product everything the per-issue announcements would have: the imported issues are searchable right away; the workspace's issues-used meter rises by the number of imported issues and agrees with a full recount; the audit log gets exactly one entry for the import — action `issues.imported`, recorded as the importer's action, subject kind `project`, subject id and project id the project's, metadata `{ count, importKey }` — and no per-issue entries. Nobody is notified about imported issues (an assignee is not told), and no outgoing webhook delivery is created for them, even for endpoints that subscribe to issue events. The application's event handlers are attached exactly as the server attaches them at start-up.\n\nUpdate any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
