     {
       "id": "EIM1-ownership-handover",
       "category": "implement",
-      "prompt": "Product change: workspace ownership can be handed over, with the new owner's consent.\n\nEvery workspace has exactly one recorded owner — the member Taskflow treats as *the* owner: the person who is alerted when someone new joins, and the one billing belongs to. Today that is fixed when the workspace is created. From now on the recorded owner can offer the workspace to another member, who takes it over by accepting.\n\nUser stories\n\n- As the workspace owner, I can offer my workspace to any other active member. The offered member gets a notification of a new kind, `ownership_handover`, inviting them to accept; nobody else is alerted. Only one offer can be open per workspace: while one is open, a further offer is refused until the open one is withdrawn, declined or lapsed. An offer lapses exactly seven days after it was made: an offer made at instant T can still be acted on before T + 7 days, and no longer from T + 7 days on.\n- As the offered member, I can accept — whatever my current role. When I accept, I become the recorded owner and hold the owner role, and the previous owner steps down to admin (if they still hold the owner role at that moment). If I already held the owner role, only the previous owner's role changes. The workspace has an owner at every instant during the handover; the existing rule that a workspace never loses its last owner must not get in the way. I can instead decline, after which nothing is pending and the owner may offer again.\n- As the owner who made an offer, I can withdraw it while it is open.\n- As any member of the workspace, I can see whether an offer is open and to whom.\n\nConsequences of acceptance (all of these must hold)\n\n- Every role change made by the handover is announced exactly as a manual role change is (the `member.role_changed` event, carrying the member, the role before and the role after), and the handover itself publishes a new domain event `organization.ownership_transferred` (`EventEnvelope & { fromUserId: UserId; toUserId: UserId }`, added to `TaskflowEventMap`), which the audit log records as an `organization.ownership_transferred` action on the organization (subject kind `organization`, subject id the workspace's, metadata `{ fromUserId, toUserId }`). Everything published during acceptance is on behalf of the accepting member.\n- Everything that follows \"the owner\" follows the new one: alerts about new members go to the new owner and no longer to the previous one; managing billing (for example changing the plan) is now the new owner's and is refused to the previous owner; and the previous owner, now an admin, can be removed from the workspace like any other admin.\n\nGuards and refusals\n\n- Only the recorded owner may offer or withdraw. Holding the owner role is not enough: a second member who has been given the owner role but is not the recorded owner is refused. Both refusals are a `PermissionDeniedError`.\n- An offer must go to an active member of the same workspace other than the owner. An unknown user, a user from another workspace or a removed member → `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`). The owner themselves → the refusal `\"self\"`.\n- Only the offered member can accept or decline. Anyone else acting on the offer gets the refusal `\"nothing_pending\"`, and so does everyone when no offer is open (accepting a second time, declining after a withdrawal, withdrawing when nothing is open). Accepting or declining an offer that has lapsed → `\"expired\"`; a lapsed offer no longer counts as open: it is not shown, it does not block a new offer, and acting on it changes nothing.\n- A new offer while one is open → `\"pending_exists\"`.\n- These refusals throw a new `OwnershipHandoverError`, exported from `src/lib/errors.ts`: it extends `Error`, has `readonly code = \"conflict\"` and `readonly reason: OwnershipHandoverRefusal`, where `export type OwnershipHandoverRefusal = \"self\" | \"pending_exists\" | \"nothing_pending\" | 