-      "prompt": "Product change: members can set themselves out of office and name a stand-in.\n\nUser stories\n\n- As a member, I can mark myself away between two instants — the start counts, the end does not — and optionally name a delegate: another active member of the same workspace, never myself. I can add a short note. Setting it again replaces it; I can also clear it. Whoever may change members' roles can set or clear anyone's absence; everyone else only their own. Anyone in the workspace can look up a member's absence.\n- While I am away (from the start up to, but not including, the end):\n  1. New assignments meant for me go to my delegate instead — whether an existing issue is assigned to me or an issue is created with me as its assignee. The issue is then the delegate's in every respect: the stored assignee, the announcements the assignment makes (events, audit), the alerts (my delegate is alerted as for any assignment; I am not), and who counts as the issue's assignee where that matters for permissions. Two exceptions: when I take an issue myself — I am the one assigning, and I assign it to me — nothing is redirected; and delegation is never chained — if my delegate is away too, the issue stays with my delegate. Without a delegate, assignments reach me as usual, and I am alerted as usual.\n  2. Overdue alerts for issues assigned to me go to my delegate instead of me. Without a delegate they reach me as before. The overdue sweep itself is unchanged.\n  3. A mention of me in a comment alerts both me and my delegate. The existing rules still apply: the comment's author is never alerted, each person gets at most one mention alert per comment (even when my delegate is mentioned directly in the same comment), and each recipient's own notification preferences apply.\n  Issues that were already mine stay mine. Nothing moves back when the absence ends; the absence simply stops applying at its end.\n- Membership: when a member is removed, their absence goes with them. When a member who is someone's delegate is removed, that delegation is dropped: the absence stays, now without a delegate, so nothing is ever redirected to someone who left.\n- Workspaces are independent: an absence set in one workspace has no effect in another, even for a user who belongs to both.\n\nRules\n\n- Validation: the end must be after the start (otherwise the request is rejected). The member must be an active member of the workspace — a removed member or a user from another workspace → `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`). The delegate, when given, must be an active member of the workspace (`NotFoundError` otherwise) and not the member themselves (rejected).\n- Permissions: setting or clearing someone else's absence without the right → `PermissionDeniedError`. An `orgId` outside the actor's workspace → `TenantScopeError`, on every operation.\n- Reading: `getAbsence` resolves to the stored absence as long as it has not ended — a scheduled absence that has not started yet is returned — and to `null` once it has ended or when none is set. Asking about someone who is not an active member → `NotFoundError`.\n- \"Away\" is decided at the moment of the action: the assignment, the sweep, the comment.\n\nAbsences are persisted; a freshly migrated database (the way the test suite builds one) must have whatever you add.\n\nAPI — add these exports:\n\nIn `src/types/member.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport interface MemberAbsence extends TenantScoped {\n  readonly userId: UserId;\n  readonly delegateUserId: UserId | null;\n  readonly startsAt: IsoTimestamp;\n  readonly endsAt: IsoTimestamp;\n  readonly note: string | null;\n}\n```\n\nIn `src/schemas/member.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport const setAbsenceSchema = z.object({\n  orgId: orgIdSchema,\n  userId: userIdSchema,\n  delegateUserId: userIdSchema.nullable().default(null),\n  startsAt: isoTimestampSchema,\n  endsAt: isoTimestampSchema,\n  note: z.string().max(200).nullable().default(null),\n});\nexport type SetAbsenceInput = z.infer<typeof setAbsenceSchema>;\n```\n\nIn `src/server/services/member-service.ts`:\n\n- `export async function setAbsence(actor: Actor, input: SetAbsenceInput): Promise<MemberAbsence>` — resolves to exactly what was stored.\n- `export async function clearAbsence(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, userId: UserId): Promise<void>` — clearing when nothing is set is fine.\n- `export async function getAbsence(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, userId: UserId): Promise<MemberAbsence | null>`\n\nUpdate any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
+      "prompt": "Product change: members can set themselves out of office and name a stand-in.\n\nUser stories\n\n- As a member, I can mark myself away between two instants — the start counts, the end does not — and optionally name a delegate: another active member of the same workspace, never myself. I can add a short note. Setting it again replaces it; I can also clear it. Whoever may change members' roles can set or clear anyone's absence; everyone else only their own. Anyone in the workspace can look up a member's absence.\n- While I am away (from the start up to, but not including, the end):\n  1. New assignments meant for me go to my delegate instead — whether an existing issue is assigned to me or an issue is created with me as its assignee. The issue is then the delegate's in every respect: the stored assignee, the announcements the assignment makes (events, audit), the alerts (my delegate is alerted as for any assignment; I am not), and who counts as the issue's assignee where that matters for permissions. Two exceptions: when I take an issue myself — I am the one assigning, and I assign it to me — nothing is redirected; and delegation is never chained — if my delegate is away too, the issue stays with my delegate. Without a delegate, assignments reach me as usual, and I am alerted as usual.\n  2. Overdue alerts for issues assigned to me go to my delegate instead of me. Without a delegate they reach me as before. The overdue sweep itself is unchanged.\n  3. A mention of me in a comment alerts both me and my delegate. The existing rules still apply: the comment's author is never alerted, each person gets at most one mention alert per comment (even when my delegate is mentioned directly in the same comment), and each recipient's own notification preferences apply.\n  Issues that were already mine stay mine. Nothing moves back when the absence ends; the absence simply stops applying at its end.\n- Membership: when a member is removed, their absence goes with them. When a member who is someone's delegate is removed, that delegation is dropped: the absence stays, now without a delegate, so nothing is ever redirected to someone who left.\n- Workspaces are independent: an absence set in one workspace has no effect in another, even for a user who belongs to both.\n\nRules\n\n- Validation: the end must be after the start (otherwise the request is rejected). The member must be an active member of the workspace — a removed member or a user from another workspace → `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`). The delegate, when given, must be an active member of the workspace (`NotFoundError` otherwise) and not the member themselves (rejected).\n- Permissions: setting or clearing someone else's absence without the right → `PermissionDeniedError`. An `orgId` outside the actor's workspace → `TenantScopeError`, on every operation.\n- Reading: `getAbsence` resolves to the stored absence as long as it has not ended — a scheduled absence that has not started yet is returned — and to `null` once it has ended or when none is set. Asking about someone who is not an active member → `NotFoundError`.\n- \"Away\" is decided at the moment of the action: the assignment, the sweep, the comment.\n\nAbsences are persisted; a freshly migrated database (the way the test suite builds one) must have whatever you add.\n\nAPI — add these exports:\n\nIn `src/types/member.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport interface MemberAbsence extends TenantScoped {\n  readonly userId: UserId;\n  readonly delegateUserId: UserId | null;\n  readonly startsAt: IsoTimestamp;\n  readonly endsAt: IsoTimestamp;\n  readonly note: string | null;\n}\n```\n\nIn `src/schemas/member.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport const setAbsenceSchema = z.object({\n  orgId: orgIdSchema,\n  userId: userIdSchema,\n  delegateUserId: userIdSchema.nullable().default(null),\n  startsAt: isoTimestampSchema,\n  endsAt: isoTimestampSchema,\n  note: z.string().max(200).nullable().default(null),\n});\nexport type SetAbsenceInput = z.infer<typeof setAbsenceSchema>;\n```\n\nIn `src/server/services/member-service.ts`:\n\n- `export async function setAbsence(actor: Actor, input: SetAbsenceInput): Promise<MemberAbsence>` — resolves to exactly what was stored.\n- `export async function clearAbsence(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, userId: UserId): Promise<void>` — clearing when nothing is set is fine.\n- `export async function getAbsence(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, userId: UserId): Promise<MemberAbsence | null>`\n\nUpdate any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine; running the installed tools is fine.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
