     {
       "id": "EIM6-workspace-deletion",
       "category": "implement",
-      "prompt": "Product change: deleting a workspace becomes a grace period, then a purge.\n\nToday deleting a workspace hides it forever and nothing can bring it back. From now on a deleted workspace is kept, inert, for `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS` days — add `export const DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS: number = 30` to `src/config/constants.ts`. During that window its owner can restore it; once the window has closed it is gone for good, and the retention sweep removes every trace of it.\n\nAdd these:\n- `src/server/services/organization-service.ts`: `export async function restoreOrganization(userId: UserId, orgId: OrgId): Promise<Organization>`. No `Actor` can be resolved for a deleted workspace, so the caller is identified by user id.\n- A new permission action `org:restore`, required role `owner`, in the permission vocabulary and the role matrix.\n- Domain events `organization.deleted` and `organization.restored` (`EventEnvelope & { slug: string }`, added to `TaskflowEventMap`), published by deletion and restoration and recorded in the audit log as the actions `organization.deleted` / `organization.restored` (subject kind `organization`, subject id = the workspace id, actor = who did it), accepted everywhere the existing audit actions are — including the zod schema that validates audit-log filters.\n\nRules\n\nDeleting\n1. `deleteOrganization` keeps its guards and its typed confirmation; it now publishes `organization.deleted`. Deleting a workspace that is already deleted throws `AlreadyArchivedError`.\n\nWhile deleted\n2. Nobody can sign in to it, and it is not listed for its members. Its name (slug) stays reserved until the purge.\n3. Nothing happens in it: the notification fan-out and `notify()` create nothing for it; a pending invitation into it cannot be accepted — the acceptance throws `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`) and the invitation stays pending; and every background job leaves it alone — no overdue alerts, no digest, its usage counters are not recomputed, an ended trial is not downgraded, its pending webhook deliveries are marked failed rather than delivered, and the retention sweep does not touch its rows. Everything it had stays exactly as it was, so a restore brings the whole workspace back.\n\nRestoring\n4. `restoreOrganization` throws `NotFoundError` when no such workspace exists, when the caller is not a member of it, or when the window has closed — from `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS × 24h` after the deletion the workspace is gone, whether or not the purge has run yet. A member who is not an owner gets `PermissionDeniedError` (the `org:restore` check). Restoring a workspace that is not deleted returns it unchanged and publishes nothing.\n5. A successful restore makes the workspace live again — sign-in, listing, invitations, notifications and every job resume — and publishes `organization.restored` with the caller as actor.\n\nPurging\n6. The retention sweep (`runCleanupArchivedJob(now)`) permanently deletes every workspace whose deletion is at least `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS × 24h` old at `now`: the workspace row and every row that belongs to it — members, invitations, projects and project members, issues, labels, comments, attachments, notifications and notification preferences, audit rows, the subscription and invoices, webhook endpoints and deliveries, search documents, and its usage counters. User accounts stay; a session that was pointed at the purged workspace simply no longer has an active workspace. Afterwards the workspace is unknown — restoring it throws `NotFoundError`, no job lists it any more — and its name can be taken by a new workspace. A live workspace, or one deleted less than the window ago, is never purged.\n\nThe sweep's existing retention work for live workspaces is unchanged. Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.\n",
+      "prompt": "Product change: deleting a workspace becomes a grace period, then a purge.\n\nToday deleting a workspace hides it forever and nothing can bring it back. From now on a deleted workspace is kept, inert, for `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS` days — add `export const DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS: number = 30` to `src/config/constants.ts`. During that window its owner can restore it; once the window has closed it is gone for good, and the retention sweep removes every trace of it.\n\nAdd these:\n- `src/server/services/organization-service.ts`: `export async function restoreOrganization(userId: UserId, orgId: OrgId): Promise<Organization>`. No `Actor` can be resolved for a deleted workspace, so the caller is identified by user id.\n- A new permission action `org:restore`, required role `owner`, in the permission vocabulary and the role matrix.\n- Domain events `organization.deleted` and `organization.restored` (`EventEnvelope & { slug: string }`, added to `TaskflowEventMap`), published by deletion and restoration and recorded in the audit log as the actions `organization.deleted` / `organization.restored` (subject kind `organization`, subject id = the workspace id, actor = who did it), accepted everywhere the existing audit actions are — including the zod schema that validates audit-log filters.\n\nRules\n\nDeleting\n1. `deleteOrganization` keeps its guards and its typed confirmation; it now publishes `organization.deleted`. Deleting a workspace that is already deleted throws `AlreadyArchivedError`.\n\nWhile deleted\n2. Nobody can sign in to it, and it is not listed for its members. Its name (slug) stays reserved until the purge.\n3. Nothing happens in it: the notification fan-out and `notify()` create nothing for it; a pending invitation into it cannot be accepted — the acceptance throws `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`) and the invitation stays pending; and every background job leaves it alone — no overdue alerts, no digest, its usage counters are not recomputed, an ended trial is not downgraded, its pending webhook deliveries are marked failed rather than delivered, and the retention sweep does not touch its rows. Everything it had stays exactly as it was, so a restore brings the whole workspace back.\n\nRestoring\n4. `restoreOrganization` throws `NotFoundError` when no such workspace exists, when the caller is not a member of it, or when the window has closed — from `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS × 24h` after the deletion the workspace is gone, whether or not the purge has run yet. A member who is not an owner gets `PermissionDeniedError` (the `org:restore` check). Restoring a workspace that is not deleted returns it unchanged and publishes nothing.\n5. A successful restore makes the workspace live again — sign-in, listing, invitations, notifications and every job resume — and publishes `organization.restored` with the caller as actor.\n\nPurging\n6. The retention sweep (`runCleanupArchivedJob(now)`) permanently deletes every workspace whose deletion is at least `DELETED_WORKSPACE_RETENTION_DAYS × 24h` old at `now`: the workspace row and every row that belongs to it — members, invitations, projects and project members, issues, labels, comments, attachments, notifications and notification preferences, audit rows, the subscription and invoices, webhook endpoints and deliveries, search documents, and its usage counters. User accounts stay; a session that was pointed at the purged workspace simply no longer has an active workspace. Afterwards the workspace is unknown — restoring it throws `NotFoundError`, no job lists it any more — and its name can be taken by a new workspace. A live workspace, or one deleted less than the window ago, is never purged.\n\nThe sweep's existing retention work for live workspaces is unchanged. Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in t