-      "prompt": "Teams want to record that one issue is blocked by another, stop blocked work from being marked done, and tell the assignee when their issue is finally unblocked. Build issue dependencies (\"blocked by\" links).\n\nAdd exactly these exports:\n\n1. A new module `src/server/services/issue-dependency-service.ts` exporting:\n   - `addBlocker(actor: Actor, input: { orgId: OrgId; issueId: IssueId; blockerId: IssueId }): Promise<IssueDependency>` — records \"`issueId` is blocked by `blockerId`\".\n   - `removeBlocker(actor: Actor, input: { orgId: OrgId; issueId: IssueId; blockerId: IssueId }): Promise<boolean>` — deletes that link; resolves `true` if a link was removed and `false` if there was none.\n   - `listBlockers(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, issueId: IssueId): Promise<readonly Issue[]>`.\n   - `class IssueBlockedError extends Error` with `readonly code = \"conflict\"` and a public `readonly blockerIds: readonly IssueId[]`.\n2. In `src/types/issue.ts`: `interface IssueDependency { orgId: OrgId; issueId: IssueId; blockerId: IssueId; createdBy: UserId; createdAt: IsoTimestamp }`.\n3. A new notification kind `\"issue_unblocked\"`, accepted everywhere the existing notification kinds are — including the zod schema that validates notification-preference updates, so users can mute it like any other kind.\n4. The links are persisted in a new table (add it to the schema and to the migrations the app runs) with a repository of their own.\n\nDefinitions: a blocker is *open* while it is not archived and its status is neither `done` nor `canceled`; otherwise it is *closed*.\n\nRules:\n- `addBlocker` / `removeBlocker` / `listBlockers`: if `orgId` is not the actor's organization, reject with the codebase's tenant-scope error. An issue id that does not exist in that organization (unknown, or belonging to another organization) is rejected with the not-found error the service layer already uses for missing rows, and nothing is stored anywhere. For `removeBlocker` only the blocked issue has to exist.\n- `addBlocker` and `removeBlocker` require the same permission as editing the blocked issue (`issueId`); otherwise reject with the codebase's permission-denied error and change nothing. `listBlockers` requires the permission to read the issue.\n- `addBlocker` rejects (any error) and stores nothing when an issue would block itself, or when the new link would create a cycle — directly (A blocked by B while B is blocked by A) or through any chain of links. If either issue is archived it rejects with the codebase's already-archived error. Adding a link that already exists is not an error: it returns the existing link and stores no duplicate. `createdBy` is the acting user.\n- `listBlockers` returns the blocker issues of `issueId` that are not archived (open or closed), ordered by issue number ascending.\n- Completion gate: changing an issue's status to `done` while it has at least one open blocker must be rejected with `IssueBlockedError`, whose `blockerIds` lists exactly the open blockers. This holds for every service path that changes an issue's status (including the board move). The issue stays unchanged and no status-change event is published. All other status changes remain allowed regardless of blockers.\n- Unblocked notification: whenever a blocker goes from open to closed — its status changes from a status other than `done`/`canceled` to `done` or `canceled`, or it is archived while its status was not `done`/`canceled` — each issue it blocks that (a) is not archived and not `done`/`canceled` itself, (b) has an assignee, and (c) now has no open blocker left, gets exactly one `issue_unblocked` notification for its assignee, with `href` equal to `/issues/<blocked issue id>` and `actorId` equal to the user who closed or archived the blocker (title and body are up to you). It goes through the app's normal notification delivery, so the usual delivery rules apply: nobody is notified about their own action, and a recipient's preferences for the kind are honoured. A closed→closed change (e.g. `done`→`canceled`, or archiving an already-closed blocker) never notifies; a blocker that is reopened and closed again unblocks — and notifies — again. Removing a link never notifies.\n- Wire the reaction into the app's normal event wiring. The test starts that wiring the way the app does at startup (`registerEventHandlers()` from `src/server/services/event-registry.ts`) and expects the notification to exist as soon as the status-change or archive call has resolved.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
