-      "prompt": "Members want personal API tokens so scripts and CI can call Taskflow on their behalf. Build them.\n\nAdd exactly these exports:\n\n1. `src/types/api-token.ts`: `type ApiTokenScope = \"read\" | \"write\"` and `interface ApiToken { id: string; orgId: OrgId; userId: UserId; name: string; scope: ApiTokenScope; prefix: string; expiresAt: IsoTimestamp; lastUsedAt: IsoTimestamp | null; revokedAt: IsoTimestamp | null; createdAt: IsoTimestamp }`. An `ApiToken` value never carries the secret token or any hash of it.\n2. `src/schemas/api-token.ts`: `createApiTokenSchema`, a zod object `{ orgId, name, scope, expiresInDays }` — `orgId` validated like the other schemas validate it, `name` trimmed and 1–80 characters, `scope` `\"read\"` or `\"write\"`, `expiresInDays` an integer from 1 to 365. Export `type CreateApiTokenInput = z.input<typeof createApiTokenSchema>`.\n3. `src/server/services/api-token-service.ts`:\n   - `createApiToken(actor: Actor, input: CreateApiTokenInput): Promise<{ token: string; apiToken: ApiToken }>`\n   - `listApiTokens(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, userId?: UserId): Promise<readonly ApiToken[]>` (`userId` defaults to the actor's own user)\n   - `revokeApiToken(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, tokenId: string): Promise<ApiToken>`\n   - `authenticateApiToken(token: string): Promise<Actor | null>`\n4. Persist tokens in a new table (schema + the migrations the app runs) behind a repository of their own, storing only a hash of the secret made with the codebase's existing token-hashing helper.\n5. Audit log: two new audit actions `\"api_token.created\"` and `\"api_token.revoked\"` and a new audit subject kind `\"api_token\"`, accepted everywhere the existing actions and subject kinds are — including the zod schema that validates audit-log filters.\n\nRules:\n- `createApiToken` validates its input with `createApiTokenSchema` (invalid input rejects with the `ZodError`, nothing stored). `input.orgId` must be the actor's organization (else the codebase's tenant-scope error). Any role may create a `read` token; a `write` token needs the role `member` or higher — a viewer asking for one gets the codebase's permission-denied error.\n- A user may hold at most 5 *usable* tokens per organization — usable meaning not revoked and not expired at that moment. Creating a sixth rejects (any error) and stores nothing; revoked and expired tokens do not count, and other users have their own allowance.\n- The secret is `tfk_` followed by a random URL-safe string (it is returned only once, from `createApiToken`). `prefix` is the first 12 characters of the secret. `expiresAt` is the creation time plus `expiresInDays` × 24 hours; `createdAt` is the creation time; `lastUsedAt` and `revokedAt` start as `null`.\n- `listApiTokens`: tenant-scope error for another organization's `orgId`. Returns the given user's tokens in that organization — revoked and expired included — newest first. Listing another user's tokens requires the role `admin` or `owner`; otherwise permission-denied.\n- `revokeApiToken`: tenant-scope error for another organization's `orgId`; a token id that does not exist in that organization is the service layer's not-found error. A user may revoke their own tokens; revoking someone else's token requires the role `admin` or `owner` (otherwise permission-denied). Revoking sets `revokedAt` to now. Revoking an already-revoked token is not an error: it returns the token unchanged (original `revokedAt`) and records nothing new.\n- `authenticateApiToken(token)` resolves `null` when the token is unknown, revoked, expired (it stops working at exactly `expiresAt`), when its user is no longer an active member of the token's organization, or when that organization has been deleted. Otherwise it resolves the `Actor` `{ userId, orgId, role }` for the token's user and organization, where `role` is `\"viewer\"` for a `read` token and, for a `write` token, the member's role *at the time of the call* (so later promotions and demotions apply). A successful call sets the token's `lastUsedAt` to now; a failed one changes nothing.\n- Audit: every token creation and every revocation (including those below) is recorded in the audit log as `api_token.created` / `api_token.revoked`, subject kind `api_token`, subject id = the token id, actor = the user who performed it. Nothing in the audit rows may contain the secret or its hash. Publish these facts as domain events and record them from the audit-log side, like the other audited actions.\n- When a member is removed from an organization, every token of theirs in that organization that is not already revoked (expired ones included) is revoked at that moment and audited with the remover as actor. Tokens already revoked keep their original `revokedAt` and are not audited again; the user's tokens in other organizations are untouched.\n- The test starts the app's event wiring the way the app does at startup (`registerEventHandlers()` from `src/server/services/event-registry.ts`) and expects all of the above to be in place as soon as the triggering service call has resolved.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
