-      "prompt": "Product change: every workspace runs on its own clock.\n\nToday Taskflow takes every calendar-day decision in UTC, and a due date counts as an instant. From now on each workspace has a time zone, and every day boundary Taskflow computes is taken on that workspace's clock.\n\nSetting\n- `OrganizationSettings` (`src/types/organization.ts`) gains `timeZone: string`, an IANA zone name such as `\"Asia/Tokyo\"`. Existing and new workspaces default to `\"UTC\"`. It is stored with the other settings: `updateOrg` (organization repository) and `updateOrganization` (organization service) accept it inside `settings`, and every organization read back carries it.\n- A zone name that `Intl` does not recognise is refused with a `RangeError`, and nothing is changed. The zod `organizationSettingsSchema` accepts `timeZone` (default `\"UTC\"`) and rejects unknown zones.\n\nHelpers in `src/lib/date.ts`\n- Add `export function localDate(value: IsoTimestamp, timeZone: string): string`, which returns the `YYYY-MM-DD` calendar date of that instant in the zone.\n- Add `export function startOfLocalDay(reference: Date, timeZone: string): IsoTimestamp`, which returns the UTC instant (an ISO string as `toIsoTimestamp` writes it) at which the zone's calendar day containing `reference` began.\n- `isOverdue` becomes `isOverdue(dueAt, reference = new Date(), timeZone = \"UTC\")`, and `digestWindow` becomes `digestWindow(digestHour, reference, timeZone = \"UTC\")`. Their rules are below.\n\nRules\n1. Due dates are whole days. An issue is overdue once the calendar day on the workspace clock that contains its `dueAt` has ended. On that day itself it is not overdue yet, whatever time of day `dueAt` names. Only open issues (not `done` or `canceled`) that are not archived and have a due date can be overdue. Everything that decides \"overdue\" follows exactly this rule, evaluated at the moment it runs: `isOverdue()`, the overdue sweep (`runOverdueIssueJob`), and the `overdueIssues` counter in the project stats returned by `getProject` and `listProjects`.\n2. Digest schedule. `settings.digestHourUtc` keeps its name, but from now on it is an hour on the workspace clock. The digest job processes a workspace during the hour when that workspace's local clock shows it (`shouldRunForOrg`).\n   - A digest covers the window returned by `digestWindow(hour, now, timeZone)`. The window ends at the most recent local `hour:00` at or before `now`: today's if it has passed, yesterday's otherwise, as today. It starts at the same local hour on the previous calendar day, so across a DST change it spans 23 or 25 hours. `digestWindow` still throws a `RangeError` for an hour outside 0-23.\n   - The job collects exactly the unread notifications created inside that window, and the digest's `windowStart`/`windowEnd` are that window. Anything newer stays unread for the next digest.\n3. Activity feed. `groupByDay(events, timeZone = \"UTC\")` (activity service) buckets events by their local calendar date. `day` is that `YYYY-MM-DD`, groups are ordered newest day first, and events keep their order within a day. The pages that render the feed pass the workspace's zone.\n4. Retention. The retention sweep's cut-off (`runCleanupArchivedJob`) is local midnight on the date that lies `retentionDays` calendar days before today's local date, not `retentionDays × 24h` before now. What the sweep does with rows older than the cut-off is unchanged.\n\nUpdate the visible tests that encode the old UTC/instant behaviour. Nothing else changes: plans and their limits, feature flags, who receives a digest, the digest's once-only delivery, and the payload of the overdue event all stay as they are.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
