     {
       "id": "UIM5-project-muting",
       "category": "implement",
-      "prompt": "Product change: members can mute a project.\n\nA member who does not want a project's day-to-day traffic can mute that project. Muting is personal: it changes only what that member receives, and the member can lift it again.\n\nAPI — add these to `src/server/services/notification-service.ts`:\n- `export async function muteProject(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, projectId: ProjectId): Promise<void>`\n- `export async function unmuteProject(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, projectId: ProjectId): Promise<void>`\n- `export async function listMutedProjectIds(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId): Promise<readonly ProjectId[]>` — the actor's own muted projects in that org.\n\n`muteProject` and `unmuteProject` change only the actor's own mutes and are idempotent. They are guarded like reading the project:\n- An `orgId` outside the actor's organization throws `TenantScopeError`.\n- A project that does not exist in that org throws `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`).\n- The actor needs the `project:read` permission on the project.\n\nMutes are persisted. The database schema is declared in the drizzle schema and also pushed from the embedded statements in `src/server/db/migrate.ts`, so keep both in step.\n\n`NotificationPayload`, the payload `notify()` takes, gains an optional `projectId?: ProjectId | null` naming the project the notification is about. Whenever `notify()` is given a project, it applies the rules below to each recipient. Without one, nothing changes.\n\nRules for a recipient who has muted project P:\n1. In-app and email notifications about P, whether they come from the domain-event fan-out or from `notify()` directly:\n   - The kinds addressed to the recipient personally — `issue_assigned`, `comment_mention` and `issue_overdue` — are still created, but never with the `email` channel. Their channels are what the recipient's preferences give, minus `email`. If nothing is left, no notification is created.\n   - Every other kind about P is not created for that recipient at all.\n   - The fan-out must tell `notify()` the project for every issue- or comment-related event, comments included.\n2. The daily digest leaves out every notification about a project that the recipient has muted at the moment the digest is built. That includes notifications created before the mute. Left-out notifications stay unread, so after an unmute they appear in a later digest while they are still inside its window. The digest's entry cap applies to what remains, and a recipient left with nothing gets no digest.\n3. Webhooks belong to the workspace, not to a member: mutes never affect outgoing webhook deliveries.\n\nNotifications that are not about a project (e.g. `member_joined`) are never affected. The existing rules stay unchanged: the actor is never notified of their own action; preferences and the `digestOnly`/plan-flag channel logic work as before; and the digest's window, recipients and once-only delivery are the same. Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
