     {
       "id": "UIM6-issue-trash",
       "category": "implement",
-      "prompt": "Product change: archived issues go to a trash. They stay restorable for a while, then they are purged for good.\n\nToday an archived issue is soft-deleted forever, and the product cannot undo it. From now on the workspace plan's `retentionDays` (`src/config/plan-limits.ts`, the same plan the retention sweep reads) is also the lifetime of an archived issue. While less than `retentionDays × 24h` has passed since its `archivedAt`, it can be restored. Once that much time has passed, it is expired: it can no longer be restored, and the retention sweep deletes it permanently.\n\nRestore\nAdd `export async function restoreIssue(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, issueId: IssueId): Promise<Issue>` to `src/server/services/issue-service.ts`.\n- Guards, the same as for archiving:\n  - an `orgId` outside the actor's organization throws `TenantScopeError`;\n  - an unknown issue throws `NotFoundError`;\n  - the actor needs the `issue:archive` permission on the issue. The existing ownership escalation applies: the issue's author or assignee may restore it whatever their role.\n- After the permission check, the restore is refused with a new `RestoreRefusedError`. Export it from `src/lib/soft-delete.ts`; it extends `Error` and has `readonly code = \"conflict\"` and a `readonly reason`. The refusals are:\n  - `\"not_archived\"` — the issue is not archived;\n  - `\"expired\"` — the issue has expired, even if the sweep has not removed it yet;\n  - `\"project_archived\"` — its project is archived;\n  - `\"quota\"` — the project has no room for it under the plan's per-project issue quota, counted exactly as `createIssue` counts it.\n- `toAppError` maps `RestoreRefusedError` to the `conflict` code. A refused restore changes nothing and publishes nothing.\n- A successful restore makes the issue live again with its original number. It publishes a new domain event `issue.restored` (`EventEnvelope & { issueId: IssueId; projectId: ProjectId }`, added to `TaskflowEventMap`). Everything that reacts to issues coming and going must react to this event too: the issues-used counter goes back up, the issue becomes searchable again, and the audit log records an `issue.restored` action for the issue.\n\nPurge\nThe retention sweep (`runCleanupArchivedJob`) keeps its existing per-workspace cut-off (`retentionDays × 24h` before `now`). For every expired issue of the workspace — all of them, in every project, however many issues the workspace has — it must:\n- delete the issue permanently, together with its label links, its comments and its attachments;\n- remove their search documents, both the issue's and its comments';\n- give the attachments' storage back: the stored storage counter drops by the megabytes those attachments were counted for (each one as its size rounded up to whole MB, as today), so the counter agrees with a full recount. The issues-used counter is not touched, because archived issues are not counted in it;\n- never reuse an issue number. After a purge, new issues in that project continue after the highest number the project has ever used, even if the purged issue held it.\n\nThe audit-log purge the sweep already does is unchanged. After an issue is purged, restoring it throws `NotFoundError`. Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.\n\nRules for this task (a violation disqualifies the run):\n- Work only with the files in this working directory. Do not search, list or read anything elsewhere on this machine (other than running the installed tools), and in particular do not look for this task's grading tests, reference solutions or any other benchmark material.\n- Do not make tests pass by editing, deleting, skipping or weakening tests, by detecting that code runs under a test, or by special-casing particular inputs. Implement the behaviour described.\n- Temporary files you create yourself under /tmp are fine.\n\nThis is an `implement` task: edit the code and stop, as for a `fix` task. Do not write a summary and do not emit an ANSWER line.",
